 ## Prepare genotypes and summaries
 
+Single-trait summary BayesC/BayesR also accepts familiar iteration and prior
+arguments:
+
+```r
+fit <- gbayes::gbayes(Glist=Glist, stat=stat, method="bayesC",
+  algorithm="mcmc", nit=100, nburn=0, seed=10)
+head(fit$stat)       # bm: native posterior mean; dm: PIP
+fit$post            # native posterior parameter table
+fit$conv            # native diagnostic list
+```
+
+Familiar calls default to one chain, 100 sweeps, zero burn-in, `h2=.5`,
+BayesC active probability `.001`, and learned slab/residual variances and
+mixture weights. `nit/nburn/nthin/nrun/seed`, `pi/gamma/vb/ve`, and
+`updateB/updateE/updatePi` adapt to native settings. Explicit conflicting
+aliases and nested settings are rejected; existing advanced calls retain
+their defaults. Familiar adaptation is limited to single-trait summaries.
+
+Summary `bm` remains in standardized native genotype/phenotype units. The
+standalone reference-LD residual policy defaults to adjustment `.9`; it differs
+from qgg's marker-specific adjustment. No independent genetic-variance draw is
+recreated. The aliases preserve native posterior and diagnostic meanings;
+single-chain output does not establish convergence or posterior equivalence.
+Use `gmap::getCredibleSets(Glist, fit, sets)` to interpret saved marginal PIPs
+with full BED LD without refitting.
+
 `gbase::gprep()` owns the qgg-style Glist contract. Sparse LD is added to the
 same object rather than creating another public descriptor.
 
